  + They take a `&GraphSession` — a **directly-opened** `GitEventStore` plus `YoAgentState::load`, built by `open_graph_session(plan, resume)` — and read `session.state()` / `session.store()` / `session.actor()` / `session.goal()`. **Superseded claim, recorded rather than erased (Day 178, #831).** This sentence used to read: *"They take the `&GaspRecorder` and read `recorder.state()` / `recorder.actor()` / `recorder.goal()` — a second `GitEventStore` on the same root is **never** opened, because a GASP repo is single-writer behind a 600s lease and a second store collides with the recorder's rather than cooperating (measured Day 165; it is the whole reason the sidecar cannot run alongside)."* The lease fact it cites is still true and is **not** what was wrong; the conclusion drawn from it was, because it answered a question nobody was asking. Two *concurrent* stores do collide — but `scripts/gasp_shim.sh` emits **one event per process**, four short-lived `yoyo gasp` invocations per session, which are **sequential and never concurrent**, so there is no second live store to collide with. What there is instead is the opposite hazard: yoagent 0.16.6's `GaspRecorder::with_store` closes any run a *previous* process left open as `"interrupted"` on **every** open, so call 2 interrupted the run call 1 opened. Measured in #831: call 3 emitted `run.finished`, `task.created` landed **into a finished run**, and `session-end` failed with `cannot finish <run>: no run is open` — **no boundary commit**, so the session's state was never pushed, **with every call still exiting 0**. That last clause is why it survived the door landing and why the test pins *ordering*, not success. The fix is what `tools/gasp-emit` always did and is confined to how the arms acquire their handle: open the store directly, and `resume_open_run()` on every arm **except** `session-start` (`gasp_cli::needs_open_run`, table-tested), so an open run survives the process boundary instead of being closed by its own next event. **`GaspRecorder` remains the correct handle for the in-process run/model/tool tier (#683 item (3)) and that path is byte-identical** — `open_recorder`, `open_recorder_from_env`, `install`, `tee_prompt` / `tee_prompt_messages` and `prompt.rs`'s `start_prompt` seam were not touched: there it is one process and one open, so interrupt-on-open is the right rule. Pinned by `tests/gasp_cli_run_ordering.rs`, which spawns **four real `CARGO_BIN_EXE_yoyo` processes** (the defect does not exist inside one process — the interrupt happens *at open* — so a single-process test calling all four arms against one handle passes on the broken code) against a scratch git repo and asserts **position**: `run.finished` is the **last** domain event, there is **exactly one** of them (two means a run was reaped mid-session), `run.started < task.created < run.finished`, and `session-end` reports a boundary commit. Ordering rather than presence is load-bearing — both columns of #831's measured table emit all the same event kinds, so an exit-code or presence-set check **passes on the broken behaviour**; the presence set is kept only as the anti-vacuous guard, so a log that recorded nothing cannot satisfy "`run.finished` is last" by having nothing to order. Verified against the defect and not only in the passing direction: reintroducing `GaspRecorder::with_store` into `open_graph_session` produced `[goal.created, run.started, run.finished, task.created, …]` with `session-end` failing and committing no boundary.
